      "note": "Decided at step 1 (said). Derivations: step 1 said: MUST, direct, because CESR line 1194 makes SAID verification a validator's obligation, and ACDC lines 134 to 147 fix the most compact form it is over. Graded at the highest, over the derivations, of the level of each.",
      "spec_conflicts": [
        {
          "line": 136,
          "quote": "First, expand a given block, verify its SAID, expand its enclosed blocks, verify their SAIDs, and so on as deep as needed or until the leaves are reached.",
          "section": "Most compact form SAID",
          "why": "Line 136 says to verify by first expanding \"a given block\" without saying block-level, which could be read as verifying the top-level SAID over the fully expanded form. The keyworded sentences do not settle it alone: line 142's MUST is about leaf blocks, and line 134's MUSTs require only that a block have one SAID and one way to compute it, which a fully expanded computation would also give. What selects the most compact form for a non-leaf block is the definitional text: line 134's depth-first algorithm and line 147, which compacts every non-leaf block \"with its SAID computed on its block-level expanded form\". The SAID verification protocol (CESR line 1194) checks the value that text defines, and line 136 read as a block-level expansion agrees with it, so the cases follow it (generators/SPEC-ISSUES.md, A-C3)."
        }
      ]
    }
